<<<
+ exciteable or excitable? time, timeous or limous? move, moveable or movable?
===
+ *exciteable* or *excitable*? time, *timeous* or *timous*? move, *moveable* or *movable*?
>>>

<<<
like, likely or likly? dote, doteard or dotard? judge, judgement or judgment?
===
like, *likely* or *likly*? dote, *doteard* or *dotard*? judge, *judgement* or *judgment*?
>>>

<<<
hinge, hingeing or hinging? singe, singeing or singing? gauge, gaugeable or gaugable?
===
hinge, *hingeing* or *hinging*? singe, *singeing* or *singing*? gauge, *gaugeable* or *gaugable*?
>>>

<<<
notice, noticeable or noticable? mouse, mousey or mousy?
===
notice, *noticeable* or *noticable*? mouse, *mousey* or *mousy*?
>>>

<<<
change, changeing or changing, changeling or changling? hie, hieing or hiing?
===
change, *changeing* or *changing*, *changeling* or *changling*? hie, *hieing* or *hiing*?
>>>

<<<
glue, gluey or gluy? due, duely or duly? blue, blueish or bluish?
===
glue, *gluey* or *gluy*? due, *duely* or *duly*? blue, *blueish* or *bluish*?
>>>

<<<
whole, wholely or wholly?
===
whole, *wholely* or *wholly*?
>>>

<<<
evening paper:— The almost ungaugable Forces which make history & forge the destinies of the race will not be hurried.
===
evening paper:— *The almost* ungaugable *Forces which make history & forge the destinies of the race will not be hurried.*
>>>

<<<
moyable
===
movable
>>>

<<<
hinging; singing; gaugable; noticable; mousy;
===
hinging; *singing*; *gaugable*; *noticable*; mousy;
>>>

<<<
changeling; hiing; gluy; duely; bluish; wholely.
===
changeling; *hiing*; *gluy*; *duely*; bluish; *wholely*.
>>>

<<<
The chief exception (gaugeable, noticeable, singeing) is that e remains even before a vowel when the soft sound of c or g is to be made possible (as before -able) or to be insisted on (as in distinguishing the participles of singe & sing).
===
The chief exception (*gaugeable, noticeable, singeing*) is that e remains even before a vowel when the soft sound of c or g is to be made possible (as before *-able*) or to be insisted on (as in distinguishing the participles of *singe & sing*).
>>>

<<<
There are no other general exceptions; duly, truly, & wholly, are individual ones merely; hieing is specially so spelt to avoid consecutive is, much as clayey has an e actually inserted to separate two ys;
===
There are no other general exceptions; *duly, truly, & wholly*, are individual ones merely; *hieing* is specially so spelt to avoid consecutive *is*, much as *clayey* has an e actually inserted to separate two *ys*;
>>>

<<<
& glucy, bluey, are due to fear that gluy, bluy, may be pronounced after buy & guy.
===
& *gluey, bluey*, are due to fear that *gluy, bluy*, may be pronounced after *buy & guy*.
>>>

<<<
gorc.
===
g or c.
>>>

<<<
change, changeling, changing, changeable; singe, singeing; hinge, hinging; trace, traceable; fake, fakable;
===
change, *changeling*, *changing*, *changeable*; singe, *singeing*; hinge, *hinging*; trace, *traceable*; fake, *fakable*;
>>>

<<<
line, linage; mite, mity 5 strive, striving; pale, palish; judge, judgement.
===
line, *linage*; mite, *mity*; strive, *striving*; pale, *palish*; judge, *judgement*.
>>>

<<<
mutilate makes -lable, -tor 5

see -ABLE l, -or.

mutiny. For inflexions see VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE, 6.
===
**mutilate** makes *-lable, -tor*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1, ^^-OR^^.

**mutiny.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE^^, 6.
>>>

<<<
mutism. So spelt; see MUTE E.
===
**mutism.** So spelt; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
mutual is a well-known trap. The essence of its meaning is that it involves the relation, æ is or does to yas yto x; & not the relation, æ is or does to z as y to z;
===
**mutual** is a well-known trap. The essence of its meaning is that it involves the relation, *x* is or does to *y* as *y* to *x*; & not the relation, *x* is or does to *z* as *y* to *z*;
>>>

<<<
from which it follows that our mutual friend Jones (meaning Jones who is your friend as well as mine), & all similar phrases, are misuses of m.
===
from which it follows that *our mutual friend Jones* (meaning Jones who is your friend as well as mine), & all similar phrases, are misuses of *m*.
>>>

<<<
An example of the mistake, which is very common, is: On the other hand, if we [i.e., the Western Powers]
===
An example of the mistake, which is very common, is: *On the other hand, if we* [i.e., the Western Powers]
>>>

<<<
merely sat with our arms folded there would be a peaceful penetration of
===
*merely sat with our arms folded there would be a peaceful penetration of*
>>>

<<<
(mileage for fear that milage may be pronounced mil-).
===
(*mileage* for fear that *milage* may be pronounced mĭl-).
>>>

<<<
The history of dispiteous is perhaps the best comment; from despite came despitous (dispi’‘tus);
===
The history of *dispiteous* is perhaps the best comment; from *despite* came *despitous* (dĭspī'tus);
>>>

<<<
when the spelling changed to despiteous (cf. the recent lineage, li‘nij), the pronunciation changed to dispi’tius (cf. the old-established lineage, li’niéj),
===
when the spelling changed to *despiteous* (cf. the recent *lineage*, lī'nĭj), the pronunciation changed to dĭspĭ'tĭus (cf. the old-established *lineage*, lĭ'nĭĕj),
>>>

<<<
& out of this came a false association with piteous, cutting the word off from its etymology & attaching it to pity instead of to spite.
===
& out of this came a false association with *piteous*, cutting the word off from its etymology & attaching it to *pity* instead of to *spite*.
>>>

